P5.4 — partial loss and recovery (the last two of the eight)
specs/readiness-recovery-10-10/tasks.md P5.4

date (UTC): 2026-08-03
commit:     c00adba
setup:      real realtime-gateway -> a proxy that 503s a WINDOW of chunks on EVERY attempt (so the
            gateway's bounded retry is exhausted, not papered over) -> real ml-inference.
            Chunks 0-2 precede the outage, 3-5 are lost, 6-11 follow it.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PARTIAL LOSS: the gateway behaves WELL, and says so

  rejected delivery attempts : 9   = 3 chunks x 3 attempts (MAX_ATTEMPTS is honoured exactly)
  realtime_gateway_chunks_forward_failed_total : 3
  per-attempt logs           : WARN "ML service returned 503 ... for chunk <id> (attempt N/3)"
  stored                     : 9/12, ids 0000 0001 0002 0006 0007 0008 0009 0010 0011

  Bounded retry, an accurate counter, and a log line per attempt naming the chunk. Nothing is
  swallowed. This is what the counter was built for and it works.

RECOVERY: PASS

  chunks after the outage window that were stored: 6/6
  The stream resumed on its own with no restart and no client involvement — the failure was
  per-chunk, not per-session, and did not poison the connection.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THE REAL FINDING, one layer up: the gap is observable but NOT ATTACHED TO THE SESSION

  `chunks_forward_failed` is a PROCESS counter on the gateway. It says "3 chunks were lost
  somewhere, at some point, for someone". It is not on the session, so:

    - platform-api does not know this session is incomplete
    - `recitation_sessions` records nothing about it
    - transcription assembles the nine chunks that exist and returns a shorter transcript
    - the aligner scores that against the FULL canonical passage

  So the words the learner DID recite during the outage are recorded as words they MISSED. Same
  outcome as the reconnect collision fixed in #323 — except there the cause was our bug, and here
  the cause is an upstream outage the system handled correctly at every individual step.

  An operator can see the loss. The learner cannot, the teacher cannot, and nothing downstream can
  decline to score a session it has no way of knowing is incomplete.

  NOT FIXED, because the right behaviour is a product decision rather than a bug fix:
    - refuse to score a session with known gaps, and tell the learner to re-record?
    - score it but mark the result provisional / not teacher-reviewable?
    - record the gap on the session and let the review UI show it?
  All three are defensible; picking one is not an engineering call. What IS clearly needed either
  way is that the gateway's per-session loss count reaches the session record — today it exists
  only in a process-wide metric.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
A CORRECTION, recorded because it nearly became a false finding

  The first run of this drill reported "chunks_forward_failed: (empty)" and "forward-failure logs:
  0", which reads as a service losing audio silently. Both were MY harness: the metric is
  `realtime_gateway_chunks_forward_failed_total` (I grepped `gateway_chunks_forward_failed`), and
  the log lines say "ML service returned 503 ... for chunk" (I grepped for the word "forward").

  That is the second time in this session a measurement blamed the product for a defect in the
  measurement. Check the instrument before writing up the reading.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
P5.4 IS NOW EIGHT OF EIGHT RUN — and the row still does not close.

  load, burst (P5.4-load-burst.log) · reconnect (P5.4-reconnect-drill.log) · long-audio
  (P5.4-long-audio.log) · duplicate-delivery · timeout · partial-loss, recovery (this file)

  Every one of these is a measurement without a target. P5.1 has not approved SLOs, a capacity
  model, or RTO/RPO, so none of these numbers can be called a pass or a fail — they are the inputs
  someone needs in order to SET those targets. The row closes when there is something to compare
  them against, and P5.7 requires an SRE to sign the evidence besides.
